Your design (quantum makes the starting tumor, then an ordinary random number generator grows it) has direct precedents in the literature. The closest one is from IBM, where a quantum computer produces only the "seed" of a map and classical code builds the rest.

## 1. Procedural generation on quantum computers (closest to your idea)

**James Wootton (IBM Research) is the key author here.**

- **"Procedural generation using quantum computation" (2020), arXiv:2007.11510** ([ar5iv](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2007.11510), [IBM](https://research.ibm.com/publications/procedural-generation-using-quantum-computation)). Encodes an image or height map into qubit amplitudes, applies small rotations, measures, and gets a varied version back. This "quantum blur" is a quantum way to vary a seed shape.
- **"A quantum procedure for map generation" (2020), arXiv:2005.10327** ([PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.10327)). Generates game terrain on IBM hardware. Press coverage at the time described IBM using quantum computers to generate Minecraft-like levels ([StatNano](https://statnano.com/world-news/77421/IBM-is-using-quantum-computers-to-generate-Minecraft-like-game-levels)). This is the clearest template for "quantum makes the start, classical code finishes it".
- **"Investigating the usefulness of Quantum Blur" (2021), arXiv:2112.01646** ([PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2112.01646)). An honest evaluation of when the quantum version adds anything over classical blur. Read it before you claim anything about your seed.
- Wootton also ran a procedural-generation game jam with Qiskit ([Qiskit x ProcJam 2020](https://decodoku.itch.io/qiskit-x-procjam-2020)), and a later talk exists on procedural generation at the dawn of fault-tolerant quantum computers ([BME](https://dtp.physics.bme.hu/node/1779)).

**Heese et al. (Fraunhofer), "Quantum Wave Function Collapse for Procedural Content Generation," arXiv:2312.13853** ([PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.13853), [Fraunhofer](https://publica.fraunhofer.de/handle/publica/481270)). Wave Function Collapse is a classical algorithm that builds structures tile by tile while respecting neighbour rules. This paper does it with qubits. It's relevant if you want the seed to obey local rules, such as "a spiculated margin cell can't sit next to a calcified one".

## 2. Mapping structure with dependencies into a circuit (your rulebook)

**Low, Yoder, Chuang (MIT), "Quantum Inference on Bayesian Networks," Phys. Rev. A 89, 062315 (2014)**, arXiv:1402.7359 ([ar5iv](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1402.7359), [MIT DSpace](https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/88648)). This is the formal basis for your design. A Bayesian network (size → type → margin) becomes a circuit of controlled rotations, and one measurement gives one sample. Cite it as the method behind your tumor rulebook encoding. There's even ready-made software: pyAgrum has a quantum Bayesian network sampling module ([docs](https://pyagrum.readthedocs.io/en/3.1.0/qBNSampling.html)).

## 3. "Entanglement reduces complexity": the real results and the trap

**The real result: quantum models of random processes need less memory.**
- **Gu, Wiesner, Rieper, Vedral, "Quantum mechanics can reduce the complexity of classical models," Nature Communications 3, 762 (2012)** ([Bristol](https://research-information.bris.ac.uk/en/publications/quantum-mechanics-can-reduce-the-complexity-of-classical-models/), [CQT](https://www.cqt.sg/?p=7652)). It proves that to simulate a step-by-step random process, a quantum simulator can remember less about the past than any classical one ([plain-language explainer](https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2012/05/120503163146.htm)). Tumor growth is exactly such a process.
- **Follow-up work (Mile Gu's group, NTU Singapore / CQT, and Griffith University)** has demonstrated this memory advantage on small photonic devices ([Griffith](https://griffith.edu.au/centre-quantum-dynamics/our-research-groups/quantum-optics-information-laboratory/quantum-complexity); [Fields Institute talk](https://www1.fields.utoronto.ca/talks/Quantum-devices-memory-reduction)). From memory, the experimental paper is Palsson et al., *Science Advances* (2017); verify before citing.

This is the one place where "quantum reduces complexity of a biological-style process" is *proven*. It suggests a later thesis chapter: is the quantum memory advantage real for a tumor-growth process?

**The trap: tensor networks.** Tensor-network methods compress big biological probability models when correlations (entanglement) are *low*:
- Kazeev et al., "Direct solution of the chemical master equation using quantized tensor trains," arXiv:1311.3143 ([PDF](https://pdf.arxiv.org/pdf/1311.3143))
- Ion et al., "Analysis of tensor methods for stochastic models of gene regulatory networks," arXiv:1910.02520 ([ar5iv](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1910.02520))
- DMRG for chemical master equations, arXiv:2501.09692 ([HTML](https://arxiv.org/html/2501.09692v1)); overview at [tensornetwork.org](https://tensornetwork.org/stoch_kin/)

These run on *classical* computers. The lesson is that if a biological model only needs a little entanglement, a classical computer can already compress it. So "use entanglement to simplify biology" only helps a quantum computer when the needed entanglement is high, which a reviewer will check. Cite these to show you know the classical competition.

## 4. The classical growth stage (after the seed)

- **Kansal, Torquato et al. (Princeton), "Cellular automaton of idealized brain tumor growth," BioSystems 55 (2000)** ([PDF](https://torquatocpanel.deptcpanel.princeton.edu/wp-content/uploads/files/publications/papers//paper-175.pdf)). The classic cellular-automaton tumor model: proliferating, dormant and dead layers growing from a few starting cells.
- **A high-performance cellular-automaton tumor growth model** ([DOI](https://doi.org/10.4236/am.2014.51017); [arXiv](https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/1309.6015)). Covers how to make it fast.

I found no papers that grow tumors on a quantum computer, so the quantum-seed-plus-classical-growth combination looks like open ground (not found, not proven absent).

## How it fits siemens-8

1. **Quantum seed, certified.** A Bayesian-network circuit (Low et al.) draws the starting tumor: size, type, margin, lobe, plus seed cells. A CHSH test in the same job certifies it.
2. **Classical growth.** A Torquato-style cellular automaton driven by a standard RNG, whose seed is taken from the quantum output. Everything stays reproducible from the signed receipt.
3. **Optional quantum variation.** Wootton's quantum blur gives the seed shape natural-looking variation.

**A key point for your defence:** a standard RNG seeded from the certified quantum bits keeps the whole growth trajectory certified. Nobody chose the start, and everything after it is deterministic and replayable from the receipt.

