Yes, papers like this exist, and one of them is almost exactly your classical stage. I put six in fake-tumors-in-ct-reading-guide.txt with links and a reading order.

**The most important find is from Johns Hopkins (Alan Yuille and Zongwei Zhou's group).**

- **"From Pixel to Cancer: Cellular Automata in Computed Tomography," MICCAI 2024** ([PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.06459)). Tumors grow pixel by pixel using cellular automata with three rules (growth, invasion, death), and are blended into real CT scans. It's rule-based, with no trained generator. Three expert radiologists judged the tumors and their growth stages realistic, and segmentation in liver, pancreas and kidney beat published benchmarks. **This is your growth stage, already validated by experts.**
- **"Label-Free Liver Tumor Segmentation," CVPR 2023** ([PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.14869)). An AI trained *only* on synthetic tumors segmented real liver tumors about as well as one trained on real ones, and doctors mixed up synthetic and real tumors. This is your strongest "fake tumors really train AI" citation for Siemens.
- **"Analyzing Tumors by Synthesis"** ([PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.06035)). A review of the whole field, covering rule-based methods and GAN or diffusion models. In their case studies, models trained with synthetic tumors matched or beat models trained only on real data.

**Also in the guide:**
- **Lungs:** the Ambrosini 2010 nodule recipe and Duke's SYN-LUNGS (512 procedural nodules in 174 digital patients).
- **CT-GAN (USENIX Security 2019)** ([PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1901.03597)). A GAN that convincingly injects or removes lung cancer in real scans. It's a strong argument *for* your signed receipts: if fakes can be indistinguishable from real scans, synthetic data needs proof of where it came from. Present it as data traceability, not security, when you pitch it to Jan.

**Where this leaves your idea:** "fake tumors train real AI" and "rule-based growth looks realistic" are both proven. What's still open, as far as I found:

- The Hopkins group works on liver, pancreas and kidney, so rule-based cellular-automaton growth in **lungs** looks untouched.
